    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense, but my students always struggle with how those two things actually interact at the same time. Like, does one cause the other, or are they just two ways of looking at the same thing? Could you maybe use a quick analogy to show how the air is physically interacting with the wing to help them visualize it?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can explain it this way: think of the wing like a canoe paddle. Imagine if you're paddling through calm water - you create a \"high-pressure\" area behind you due to resistance, and a \"low-pressure\" area in front of you, allowing you to move forward. Now, picture the wing as a paddle that creates a similar pocket of low pressure above and a pocket of high pressure below as it moves forward. Both Bernoulli's principle (low pressure above) and Newton's third law (upward force counteracts downward force) describe the same fundamental interaction between the air and the wing, allowing it to generate lift and stay aloft.",
      "source": "target",
