    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You could use another analogy to focus on the force and interaction: think of the air moving over the wing like a crowd of people pushing forward on a sidewalk. At the crowd's front edge (the leading edge of the wing), some people are pushed back, reducing their forward pressure, while the people at the back (the trailing edge) are pushed forward, increasing their pressure. According to Newton's third law, the \"pushed-back\" people push the street with an equal force (the air flows downward in a reaction), and the \"pushed-forward\" people push the wing upward with the same force. In this scenario, both the increased velocity (Bernoulli's principle) and Newton's third law illustrate how the air interacts with the wing to create lift.",
